data: {"type":"handoff","handoff":{"text":"{\"v\":1,\"hash\":\"b9c4a57381ea9ddb699ee666755c58f30b5b91b61b304330fddf621f633a2b36\",\"at\":1792407513622,\"retried\":[],\"merged\":{\"byChunk\":[{\"chunkId\":1,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"1.1\",\"text\":\"Apollo 11 (July 16–24, 1969) was the American spaceflight that first landed humans on the Moon and the fifth crewed mission of NASA's Apollo program.\"},{\"id\":\"1.2\",\"text\":\"The crew was Commander Neil Armstrong, Command Module Pilot Michael Collins, and Lunar Module Pilot Edwin 'Buzz' Aldrin, all on their second and final spaceflight.\"},{\"id\":\"1.3\",\"text\":\"The Saturn V launched Apollo 11 from Kennedy Space Center, Florida, on July 16 at 13:32 UTC.\"},{\"id\":\"1.4\",\"text\":\"The command module housed the three astronauts and was the only part to return to Earth; the service module provided propulsion, electrical power, oxygen, and water.\"},{\"id\":\"1.5\",\"text\":\"Armstrong and Aldrin landed the LM Eagle in the Sea of Tranquility on July 20 at 20:17 UTC while Collins remained in lunar orbit aboard CM Columbia.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Apollo 11\",\"NASA\",\"Neil Armstrong\",\"Michael Collins\",\"Edwin \\\"Buzz\\\" Aldrin\",\"Saturn V\",\"Kennedy Space Center\",\"Lunar Module\",\"Eagle\",\"Sea of Tranquility\",\"Columbia\",\"Apollo program\"]},{\"chunkId\":2,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"2.1\",\"text\":\"Armstrong walked on the Moon about six hours after landing, with Aldrin following nineteen minutes later.\"},{\"id\":\"2.2\",\"text\":\"The two spent about two and a half hours on the surface, planting a flag, speaking with Nixon, deploying instruments, and collecting 21.5 kg of lunar material.\"},{\"id\":\"2.3\",\"text\":\"The crew rejoined Collins in lunar orbit and returned to Earth, splashing down in the Pacific Ocean on July 24.\"},{\"id\":\"2.4\",\"text\":\"Apollo 11 culminated the Space Race, a Cold War geopolitical competition between the United States and the Soviet Union.\"},{\"id\":\"2.5\",\"text\":\"Kennedy set the goal of landing a man on the Moon and returning him safely before the decade ended; the program recovered from the fatal Apollo 1 fire in January 1967.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Armstrong\",\"Aldrin\",\"Collins\",\"Richard Nixon\",\"Apollo 11\",\"Space Race\",\"United States\",\"Soviet Union\",\"John F. Kennedy\",\"Apollo 1\",\"Mercury\",\"Gemini\"]},{\"chunkId\":3,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"3.1\",\"text\":\"The Soviet Union, despite early spaceflight leads, could not match the Saturn V rocket, and its uncrewed probe Luna 15 crashed on the Moon on July 21.\"},{\"id\":\"3.2\",\"text\":\"Armstrong's moonwalk was broadcast live to an estimated 600 million viewers, roughly one-fifth of the world's population.\"},{\"id\":\"3.3\",\"text\":\"Armstrong declared 'That's one small step for [a] man, one giant leap for mankind' upon stepping onto the lunar surface.\"},{\"id\":\"3.4\",\"text\":\"Lunar samples led to the identification of three previously unrecognized minerals, and surface instruments returned data for years afterwards.\"},{\"id\":\"3.5\",\"text\":\"The crew were honoured with ticker-tape parades, a world goodwill tour, and the Presidential Medal of Freedom; Columbia is preserved at the National Air and Space Museum.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Soviet Union\",\"Luna 15\",\"Neil Armstrong\",\"Buzz Aldrin\",\"Saturn V\",\"Columbia\",\"Eagle\",\"National Air and Space Museum\",\"Presidential Medal of Freedom\",\"Cold War\"]},{\"chunkId\":4,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"4.1\",\"text\":\"Soviet Union launched Sputnik 1 on October 4, 1957, the first artificial satellite, surprising the world and fueling fears about Soviet capabilities.\"},{\"id\":\"4.2\",\"text\":\"Sputnik's success suggested the USSR could potentially deliver nuclear weapons over intercontinental distances, challenging American claims of superiority.\"},{\"id\":\"4.3\",\"text\":\"Sputnik sparked the Sputnik crisis and ignited the Space Race between the two superpowers.\"},{\"id\":\"4.4\",\"text\":\"President Dwight D. Eisenhower responded by creating NASA and initiating Project Mercury, which aimed to place a man into Earth orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"4.5\",\"text\":\"Yuri Gagarin became the first person in space and first to orbit Earth on April 12, 1961; Alan Shepard's May 5, 1961 flight was suborbital.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Soviet Union\",\"Sputnik 1\",\"United States\",\"Dwight D. Eisenhower\",\"National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA)\",\"Project Mercury\",\"Yuri Gagarin\",\"Alan Shepard\",\"John F. Kennedy\",\"Space Race\"]},{\"chunkId\":5,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"5.1\",\"text\":\"Kennedy, Eisenhower's successor, chose a challenge beyond existing rockets' capabilities so both countries would start from an equal position.\"},{\"id\":\"5.2\",\"text\":\"A crewed Moon mission was presented as the challenge that would serve this purpose.\"},{\"id\":\"5.3\",\"text\":\"On May 25, 1961, Kennedy told Congress the nation should commit to landing a man on the Moon and returning him safely before the 1960s end.\"},{\"id\":\"5.4\",\"text\":\"Kennedy said no single space project would be more impressive to mankind or more important for long-range space exploration, and none more difficult or expensive.\"},{\"id\":\"5.5\",\"text\":\"Kennedy proposed accelerating lunar spacecraft development, developing alternate liquid and solid fuel boosters, and funding other engines and unmanned exploration.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Kennedy\",\"Eisenhower\",\"United States Congress\",\"Moon\",\"Earth\"]},{\"chunkId\":6,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"6.1\",\"text\":\"Kennedy said that going to the Moon would be an entire nation's effort, since all of us must work to put one man there.\"},{\"id\":\"6.2\",\"text\":\"On September 12, 1962, Kennedy gave a speech to about 40,000 people at Rice University football stadium in Houston, Texas.\"},{\"id\":\"6.3\",\"text\":\"Kennedy said outer space has no strife, prejudice, or national conflict yet, and its hazards are hostile to all.\"},{\"id\":\"6.4\",\"text\":\"Kennedy argued that its conquest deserves the best of all mankind and its opportunity for peaceful cooperation may never come again.\"},{\"id\":\"6.5\",\"text\":\"Kennedy answered 'why the Moon?' by comparing it to climbing the highest mountain, flying the Atlantic, and Rice playing Texas, concluding 'We choose to go to the Moon.'\"}],\"entities\":[\"Kennedy\",\"Rice University\",\"Houston, Texas\",\"Moon\",\"Texas\"]},{\"chunkId\":7,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"7.1\",\"text\":\"Kennedy's 'not because they are easy, but because they are hard' rationale framed landing on the Moon this decade as a goal to organize energies and skills.\"},{\"id\":\"7.2\",\"text\":\"The proposed Moon program faced widespread opposition; mathematician Norbert Wiener of MIT dubbed it a 'moondoggle'.\"},{\"id\":\"7.3\",\"text\":\"The effort to land a man on the Moon was already named Project Apollo.\"},{\"id\":\"7.4\",\"text\":\"Kennedy proposed a joint Moon landing to Soviet Premier Nikita Khrushchev in June 1961, but Khrushchev declined.\"},{\"id\":\"7.5\",\"text\":\"Kennedy repeated the joint lunar expedition proposal at the UN General Assembly on September 20, 1963; the joint idea was abandoned after his death.\"},{\"id\":\"7.6\",\"text\":\"An early crucial decision was choosing lunar orbit rendezvous over direct ascent and Earth orbit rendezvous.\"}],\"entities\":[\"John F. Kennedy\",\"Norbert Wiener\",\"Massachusetts Institute of Technology\",\"Project Apollo\",\"Nikita Khrushchev\",\"Soviet Union\",\"United Nations General Assembly\",\"lunar orbit rendezvous\",\"direct ascent\",\"Earth orbit rendezvous\"]},{\"chunkId\":8,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"8.1\",\"text\":\"In July 1962, NASA head James Webb announced that lunar orbit rendezvous would be used for the Apollo mission.\"},{\"id\":\"8.2\",\"text\":\"The Apollo spacecraft had three major parts: the command module, the service module, and the lunar module.\"},{\"id\":\"8.3\",\"text\":\"Only the command module, which housed the three astronauts, returned to Earth.\"},{\"id\":\"8.4\",\"text\":\"The service module supplied the command module with propulsion, electrical power, oxygen, and water.\"},{\"id\":\"8.5\",\"text\":\"The lunar module had descent and ascent stages, allowing a single Saturn V launch; Apollo relied on Gemini technologies and semiconductor advances like MOSFETs and silicon ICs.\"}],\"entities\":[\"James Webb\",\"NASA\",\"Apollo\",\"command module\",\"service module\",\"lunar module\",\"Saturn V\",\"Project Gemini\",\"Interplanetary Monitoring Platform\",\"Apollo Guidance Computer\"]},{\"chunkId\":9,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"9.1\",\"text\":\"The Apollo 1 fire on January 27, 1967 killed astronauts Gus Grissom, Ed White, and Roger B. Chaffee and abruptly halted Project Apollo.\"},{\"id\":\"9.2\",\"text\":\"In October 1968, Apollo 7 evaluated the command module in Earth orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"9.3\",\"text\":\"In December 1968, Apollo 8 tested the command module in lunar orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"9.4\",\"text\":\"In March 1969, Apollo 9 tested the lunar module in Earth orbit; in May, Apollo 10 conducted a lunar orbit 'dress rehearsal'.\"},{\"id\":\"9.5\",\"text\":\"By July 1969, all was ready for Apollo 11 to make the final step onto the Moon.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Project Apollo\",\"Apollo 1\",\"Gus Grissom\",\"Ed White\",\"Roger B. Chaffee\",\"Apollo 7\",\"Apollo 8\",\"Apollo 9\",\"Apollo 10\",\"Apollo 11\"]}],\"entities\":[\"Apollo 11\",\"NASA\",\"Neil Armstrong\",\"Michael Collins\",\"Edwin \\\"Buzz\\\" Aldrin\",\"Saturn V\",\"Kennedy Space Center\",\"Lunar Module\",\"Eagle\",\"Sea of Tranquility\",\"Columbia\",\"Apollo program\",\"Armstrong\",\"Aldrin\",\"Collins\",\"Richard Nixon\",\"Space Race\",\"United States\",\"Soviet Union\",\"John F. Kennedy\",\"Apollo 1\",\"Mercury\",\"Gemini\",\"Luna 15\",\"Buzz Aldrin\",\"National Air and Space Museum\",\"Presidential Medal of Freedom\",\"Cold War\",\"Sputnik 1\",\"Dwight D. Eisenhower\",\"National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA)\",\"Project Mercury\",\"Yuri Gagarin\",\"Alan Shepard\",\"Kennedy\",\"Eisenhower\",\"United States Congress\",\"Moon\",\"Earth\",\"Rice University\",\"Houston, Texas\",\"Texas\",\"Norbert Wiener\",\"Massachusetts Institute of Technology\",\"Project Apollo\",\"Nikita Khrushchev\",\"United Nations General Assembly\",\"lunar orbit rendezvous\",\"direct ascent\",\"Earth orbit rendezvous\",\"James Webb\",\"Apollo\",\"command module\",\"service module\",\"Project Gemini\",\"Interplanetary Monitoring Platform\",\"Apollo Guidance Computer\",\"Gus Grissom\",\"Ed White\",\"Roger B. Chaffee\",\"Apollo 7\",\"Apollo 8\",\"Apollo 9\",\"Apollo 10\"],\"findingCount\":9}}","seal":"24861b421eafaedb9b20582d9dd288e267c1f6068cefe14b9be7000ffc3f284e"}}

